(Kampong Cham): The Documentary Team of the Cham Silk Project or the Color of Memory of the Documentation Center of Cambodia organized a study visit to three representatives of Cham women working in silk weaving in a community in Koh Sotin district, Kampong Cham province. There are two days from July 16-17, 2024 in Phnom Penh and Takeo.
In Phnom Penh, a representative of Cham women weavers met with Ülkü Kocaefe, Turkish Ambassador to Cambodia to report on the progress of the Cham Silk Project and discuss future long-term strategic plans. The Cham silk project is very important in preserving the remaining Cham cultural traditions from the Khmer Rouge regime and requires the participation of the younger generation as well as creating competition.
After the meeting in Phnom Penh, representatives of the three Cham weavers visited the Takeo Silk Weaving Training Center based in Takeo's Samrong District. The center is sponsored by Maybank. The purpose of this visit is to expand the understanding of the Cham women weavers' representatives regarding handicrafts and to make their products more unique and distinctive.
Cham women representatives are interested in this silk weaving skills training project because it provides opportunities for the Samrong district community, especially women and girls, which provides knowledge, skills and resources to the community. Afterwards, the Cham women representatives shared their weaving skills with the managers at the Silk Weaving Training Center in Takeo Province.
The representatives of these Cham women became more confident and energetic after this visit and had a long-term vision for the future regarding silk weaving.
(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries and the Ministry of Environment have issued a press release regarding the discovery of a total of five mountain crocodile nests in the Cardamom Mountain National Park.
According to the announcement, in mid-May 2024, the Ministry of Environment, Forestry Administration of the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, wildlife and plant and local communities found a total of five mountain crocodile nests in the national park. The Cardamom Mountains, the largest nesting nest ever in more than 20 years.
The five nests have a total of 106 eggs, of which 66 are fertile. The nests are carefully guarded by park rangers and local communities. As a result, a total of 60 mountain crocodiles were successfully hatched from their nests between June 27 and 30, 2024.
The findings show that the area is an important habitat for wild crocodiles and gave hope for the species' growth in nature.
It should also be noted that mountain crocodiles are the most endangered species in nature and there are only about 1,000 in the world, of which Cambodia is considered to have more mountain crocodiles than in the world. More than 300.
