Following the post-epidemic recovery, driven primarily by domestic demand, exports are recovering and boosting regional economic growth. Strong global demand for electronics, especially semiconductors used for high-tech applications and artificial intelligence, is boosting the exports of some Asian countries.
"Most of the countries in Asia and the Pacific are seeing higher growth in the second half of last year," said Albert Park, chief economist at ADB. The fundamentals of the region remain strong, but policymakers still need to be aware of a number of risks that could affect this outlook, including uncertainty regarding election results in major economies, decision-making, interest rates and uncertainty. Geopolitical tensions.
While inflation is slowing to pre-epidemic levels across the region, price pressures continue to rise in some countries. Food inflation remains high in South Asia, Southeast Asia and the Pacific, in part due to bad weather and restrictions on food exports in some countries.
The growth forecast for the People's Republic of China (PRC), the region's largest economy, has been maintained at 4.8 percent this year. Continued recovery in service use, as well as higher-than-expected exports and industrial activity, is supporting growth, despite the struggling PRC real estate sector. The government introduced additional policy measures in May to support the real estate market.
The outlook for India, the region's fastest growing economy, remains unchanged at 7.0% for fiscal 2024. India's industrial sector is projected to grow strongly, driven by manufacturing and high demand for construction. Agriculture is expected to recover as monsoon forecasts are higher than usual with investment demand remaining strong, led by public investment.
The Asian Development Bank (ADB) is committed to achieving environmental prosperity, resilience and sustainability in the Asia-Pacific region in line with its efforts to eradicate extreme poverty. Founded in 1966, the bank is governed by 68 member countries, of which 49 are from the region.
