But the IMF has given an optimistic outlook on consumption due to the expectation that the high pay rise offered by the company in this year's spring wage negotiations will boost household incomes.
"In Japan, a strong wage increase is expected to support the shift in private consumption, which begins in the second half of the year," Reuters reported, citing a report on the global economic outlook released by the IMF. .
The IMF said it expects Japan's economy to grow 0.7 percent this year, down from 0.2 percent in its April forecast of 1.9 percent in 2023. For 2025, the IMF has maintained its forecast for Japan's economy to grow by 1 percent.
The recent weakness in consumption has been cited by some analysts as a factor that could prevent the Bank of Japan (BOJ) from raising interest rates too quickly from the current near-zero level.
Bank of Japan Governor Kazuo Ueda is optimistic about the outlook for consumption, saying it is likely to recover as wages rise more widely and boost household purchasing power.
The Bank of Japan is likely to cut its annual growth forecast for July in July, but inflation will remain within its target range of 2% for the next few years, sources told Reuters. Opportunity to raise interest rates this month.
