(Phnom Penh): The National Bank of Cambodia and related institutions, together with development partners, have been paying close attention to mainstreaming financial literacy and promoting the prudent and responsible use of financial services for the people. All citizens, especially children, women and local people.
In fact, on July 16, 2024, the National Bank of Cambodia organized a workshop on "Microfinance in Cambodia" in Ratanakkiri province with the participation of Mr. Rath Sovannarak, Deputy Governor of the National Bank of Cambodia.
Mr. Rath Sovannarak said that the microfinance sector in Cambodia has shown great development and growth and has been playing an important role in contributing to the economic and social development of Cambodia, promoting the level of financial environment, reducing inequality in Access to financial services and poverty alleviation of local people.
He said the sector has continued to expand its scope of operations sustainably and confidently, and has continued to develop new financial services to meet the financial needs effectively, safeguarded and environmentally friendly.
He added, however, that the sector was facing accusations and concerns from some civil society organizations regarding over-lending, which pushed impoverished households into over-indebtedness. Threats and forced families to reduce their diets, sell land, engage in illegal migration, or encourage drop out of school and the oppression of child labor in order to make ends meet.
He said the main causes of the allegations were: lending and illegal pawnshops, low financial literacy, gaps in compliance with laws and regulations. Evaluate lending, focusing on collateral, high fees and penalties, and the performance of employees without professional ethics.
Mr. DC Sitha, Board of Directors of the Cambodian Microfinance Association, said that the National Bank of Cambodia has always taken care to support the welfare and sustainability of the banking and financial sector in Cambodia, while this sector has been playing an important role in The goal is to contribute to the promotion and promotion of sustainable economic growth in Cambodia.
He said the sector provided employment opportunities for people in urban and rural areas and improved their lives. More importantly, the microfinance sector in Cambodia also helps increase income for people, develop small and medium enterprises by providing loans for capital to expand business and agriculture. Continue to disseminate the Royal Government's priority policy program in Battambang province
