The following is a letter of thanks to Mr. Nhong Sauly and his wife: Samdech Techo Hun Sen told the Japanese senator that he will never change his views on the value of peace before all, if there is no peace, there is no democracy and Human rights
Samdech Techo's emphasis was made during a working meeting with Mr. Yamaguchi Natsuo this morning, July 16, 2024, at the Senate Palace, as the Japanese senator recalled Cambodia's past.
During the visit, he also discussed with Heng Ratana on cooperation in continuing mine action in Cambodia.
For this visit, it is more than 32 years since he first came to Cambodia, he was really surprised to see that Cambodia is prosperous, especially Phnom Penh is developing rapidly and completely peaceful. .
He stressed that he adheres to the principle of valuing peace before anything else, because without peace, there is no democracy and no human rights.
He added that many countries are shouting for democracy and human rights, regardless of the peace in the country, which is like looking at a single tree or a tree, not looking at the whole forest.
"Peace is priceless because there is peace so we can have everything we have today," he said.
Samdech Techo continued that, obviously, for Japan's assistance in demining, if there is no peace, it is not possible to clear mines, because the more we clear mines, the more they will be added.
Samdech Techo stated that democracy and human rights must go hand in hand with the rule of law in order to maintain peace. Thus, maintaining a balance of these three factors, the country will have real development and peace.
Despite the large amount of assistance provided, Samdech Techo observed that the investment of Japanese companies in Cambodia is still limited. Samdech continued that the Royal Government of Cambodia under the leadership of Samdech Prime Minister is preparing a better investment environment.
Samdech wants Japanese companies to increase investment in Cambodia to boost Cambodia's economic growth. At the same time, Samdech is very pleased with the upgrading of Cambodia-Japan bilateral relations to a comprehensive strategic partnership and is proud to have worked with Japan for the past 34 years.
The prince also expressed his pride in opening the door for Japan to send peacekeepers to Cambodia first.
Mr. YAMAGUCHI thanked Samdech Techo and clearly recorded his wise views on the values of absolute peace, peace, demining, development of the country and the rule of law.
In promoting Japanese investment in Cambodia, he will bring this content to the Government of Japan and especially to the Minister of Public Works of Japan, a member of the Komeito Party, to build more and more connected infrastructure in Cambodia, which is an important factor for investors. .
