The Extraordinary Chambers in the Courts of Cambodia (ECCC) was established under an agreement between the Royal Government of Cambodia and the United Nations in June 2003, but is an independent tribunal. Neither the government nor the United Nations to prosecute the crimes committed by the top leaders in Cambodia during the Khmer Rouge regime from 1975-1979.
Achievements of the ECCC These noble values are inseparable from the wise leadership of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the former Prime Minister and current President of the Senate, who initiated the Extraordinary Chambers in the Courts of Cambodia, with the participation of the United Nations to provide justice. To the victims in order to ensure peace and national unity as part of Samdech Techo's win-win policy framework.
ECCC It has also established an international model tribunal to try genocidal crimes that use the jurisdiction of the host country, and use a combination of common law and civil law, and reflect the potential of Cambodia's judicial process for national unification. Reconciliation achieves justice for the victims and ensures the sustainability of peace for the motherland.
