Monks and Buddhists from Thailand bring Vesa candles and relics to monks at 11 pagodas in Siem Reap
(Siem Reap): Monks and Buddhists from Prasat district, Surin province, Thailand bring Preah Vossa candles and relics to monks at 11 pagodas in Siem Reap city, Siem Reap province. Vesa candles and relics Teacher Kan Pricha (called Mr. Tapik) on the afternoon of July 14, 2024 on the occasion of the New Year. The ceremony was held at Wat Svay Romeat.
Preah Noch Vuthy said that this tradition has always been a tradition, and both the King and the common people have provided various sacrificial equipment for the monks who are waiting for the rain to benefit the nation. In those sacrifices, "candles" are found, which are important objects that are not prescribed in the Buddhist scriptures, but Khmer practice is still a tradition today.
Preah Noch Vuthy continued that the burning of candles or lanterns for the day of the Vesak is for the benefit of the monks who need to perform the Dharma and perform ablutions at night during the Vesa. The light that burns during the rainy season is also considered to be the light that flashes to the human body.
In the case of Preah Vossa candles, in each pagoda, two pairs of vases and 1,500 baht suffixes are given. Buddhist primary school at Wat Svay Romeat. Samdech Men Sam An Presents Preah Vossa Candles to 8 Monks in Svay Rieng Province
(Svay Rieng): Samdech Kitti Sangkhab Dr. Men Sam An, His Majesty the King's Personal Adviser, presented Vesa candles to monks who have been waiting for Preah Vossa for eight quarters at eight pagodas organized at Koy Trabek pagoda in Koy Trabek commune. Svay Rieng city, Svay Rieng province, in the morning of July 15, 2024.
Monks must stay in the pagoda compound. However, monks can accept religious invitations and travel during the day, but can not travel far beyond the day and night. Buddhists usually offer large rain candles, which are lit throughout the day and night during festivals, as well as oil, incense, and other food items for monks.
The festival also brings together Buddhists to pay homage to Preah Ratanatray and invite monks to prosper in order to dedicate the great merits to the seven relatives and ancestors who have passed away to receive the fruit of the monks who were born. About today's festival in the traditional way of Buddhism, the state religion.
