The following is the message of the Chief Bishop of Wat Ounalom to Lok Chumteav Dr. Pech Chanmony Hun Manet: Understanding Business: National Policy for the Development of Electric Vehicles will Attract Investment Attraction in Cambodia
(Phnom Penh): The Royal Government of Cambodia has identified the electric vehicle sector as one of the sectors with high potential to contribute to the acceleration of economic base diversification in the future, while estimating that electric vehicles will contribute about 50 % Of new vehicle sales worldwide by 2035 and about 61% by 2040.
In order to attract investors to invest in opening electric vehicle assembly plants in Cambodia, the Royal Government has launched a national policy on the development of the electric vehicle sector, which is the long-term vision of the Royal Government of Cambodia to focus on investment promotion. Utilization and development of electric vehicle ecosystem step by step according to the availability and resources of the Royal Government.
In the past, Cambodia did not have comprehensive policies and incentives, both fiscal and non-fiscal, to support the development of the electric vehicle sector like other countries in the region. These factors provide uncertainty for consumers and investors.
According to the National Electric Vehicle Development Policy, attracting investment and providing a favorable business environment for investors is important in absorbing the technology, skills and resources that Cambodia is in high demand today. Therefore, the national policy decided to set out the following measures:
1. Strengthen the attractiveness and convenience of the business environment in the field of electric vehicles by manipulation
Review and improve on fiscal and non-fiscal incentives for investment in the assembly and production of electric vehicle components.
3-Locate special economic zones for electric vehicle clusters and build support infrastructure
5-Examine the possibility of providing incentives for the conversion of fuel-efficient vehicles into electric vehicles and the arrangement of vehicle demolition sites, recycling and reuse of batteries.
6. Examine the possibility of regulatory incentives for banking and financial institutions to provide loans to investors within the framework of green credit policy.
7. Organize regular dissemination workshops with the private sector on the government's incentive mechanism to promote investment in electric vehicles.
8. Strengthen industry monitoring and evaluation mechanisms after receiving incentives.
At the same time, Cambodia has the ability to produce simple components for general vehicles. In Cambodia, for example, the local assembly industry, such as wiring and tire manufacturers, to supply the car assembly industry in the region. Based on this industry, Cambodia can seize the important opportunity to connect the global electric vehicle assembly line, which is expected to increase by 2030.
At the same time, as most electric vehicle components are more complex, moderately complex, and lighter than conventional vehicle components, Cambodia may have to compete for cost for the production of electric vehicle components in the coming century.
Through the formulation of this policy, the Royal Government has also considered strengthening the supply chain of components to the provision of support infrastructure, including power stations and interconnections, as well as various incentive mechanisms to promote the use of investment and The development of the electric vehicle sector as well.
