(Phnom Penh): Mr. Tep Asarith, spokesman for the Ministry of Information, said that in order to protect themselves, their families and society from the dangers of false information, every citizen must adhere to certain principles.
Those principles include:
First, give up the habit of sharing information too quickly or sharing it without having read it easily and without first verifying the information.
Second, do not trust the source of information from social media alone, that is, read and make a habit of verifying with other sources such as traditional media, such as newspapers, television, radio, news websites and official digital media. A profession that respects professional ethics or other official sources of information to ensure that it is factual, accurate, reliable before distributing.
Third, embrace traditional media and professional media agencies with professional ethics as a priority source for daily news coverage and encourage others to do the same.
He added that in order to be able to identify whether a piece of information is fake or not, one can stand on some basic principles:
First, it is necessary to find the source of the information and find out if it was published by another source to be able to verify to avoid it being just a rumor or just hearing.
Third, always be wary of any advertisements made for the purpose of deception or deception, which usually seems to encourage the recipient to do something immediately or urgently so as not to be late with rewards or benefits. The stem is a predicate.
Fourth, the absence of core elements or main characters in the information embedded in bias, with political manipulation to distort false information, to mislead public opinion, destroy reputation or cause unrest, 5) before believing any information, especially on Social media, please read and verify that information first and consider carefully before deciding to share it with others.
