General knowledge: Who discovered the radioactive substance?
(Phnom Penh): Radium (Ra)'s radiation was initially used to treat tumors and cancer, but it was later discovered that radium can be harmful to human health due to radiation levels and reactions. Its chemistry is too strong. Who discovered the radioactive substance?
Pierre Curie and Mary were soon married in 1895. Mary and Pierre then formed a team to do a lot of research. Her previous research with her husband was often conducted under difficult conditions, poor laboratory setup, and they both had to teach a lot for a living.
Radium is used to treat tumors. Curietherapy has begun. The Curie family won the Nobel Prize in Physics for their discovery. They shared the award with the French physicist Antoine, who discovered the radioactive nature. Prolonged exposure to radioactive elements weakened Pierre, respectively.
In 1906 Pierre was hit by a horse-drawn carriage and died. Her husband's death saddened her, but she did not lose her temper. He continued his work on radioactive substances and won the Nobel Prize in Chemistry for his analysis of radium and its chemical properties.
In 1914 she helped establish the Radium Institute in Paris, France, and was its first director. Mary is known as Curie to this day. He is highly respected and admired by scientists around the world.
When World War I broke out, she thought X-rays would help determine the location of bullets and facilitate surgery. It was important not to move during the injury, so she built an X-ray vehicle and trained 150 female caregivers. "We have to work," he said.
In 1929, President Hoover of the United States awarded $ 50,000 in donations from fellow American scientists to purchase radium for laboratory use in Warsaw. Curie holds an honorary degree in medical science and law and is an honorary member of the Society of Scholars around the world.
