40,000 union leaders sue for incitement to discriminate under the Penal Code
(Phnom Penh): The Cambodian Confederation of Trade Unions, representing seven unions with more than 40,000 workers, has filed another lawsuit against Central Officer Khun Tharo and his accomplices for inciting discrimination and public defamation under the Penal Code. Articles 494, 496 and 305 of the Penal Code of the Kingdom of Cambodia.
Hang Ravy, president of the Cambodian Confederation of Trade Unions, said that on June 27, 2024, his union had filed a lawsuit against Khun Tharo for inciting discrimination and public defamation, demanding compensation. Hundreds of millions of riels. The second lawsuit was filed on July 8, in which he kept the content of the complaint the same, but also sued his partner Khun Tharo, demanding 1 billion riel in damages and damages to the union.
He said the lawsuit was filed because Khun Tharo and his partner released a report on June 4 based on interviews with 10 of the 14 unions registered with the Better Factories Cambodia program (BFC). Negatively speaking, freedom of association in Cambodia was completely destroyed. But after reactions from many unions, Khun Tharo explained that the report was just a shadowy report that did not completely cover the situation in Cambodia.
The president of the Cambodian Trade Union Confederation of Trade Unions stated that there are up to 6,272 unions in Cambodia, but the provocative content of the report is to divide the colors of these unions into two, first, independent unions, which have only 10 of the 14 unions. Provided interviews and information for the basis of report production. Second, it is arbitrarily accused of having yellow unions other than the above independent unions and unions affiliated with the company.
"This racial segregation is a malicious intent to disrupt the unity of the unions in Cambodia and to discriminate against unions. It is stated and punished in accordance with the provisions of Article 494 and Article 496 of the Penal Code of the Kingdom of Cambodia.
