At the same time, small-scale food handicrafts, which used to be traditionally produced and processed, are gradually evolving to produce new technologies in line with advances in technology and market demand that make their products more marketable.
Starting as a pork seller in a regular market and owning a pig farm, Song Lihoun started her 2016 beef and pork sausage business, producing only small quantities through Handmade in a family style.
Later, as market demand increased, she increased production by switching to machines, which made production more hygienic. After the transition from hand-made to sausage-making machines of the Siem Reap-based sausage and dry-meat craft owned by the middle-aged entrepreneur, it has increased more than 10 times, from 20 kilograms a day to 300 to 400 kilograms. Grams per day.
Currently, she is expanding the market, especially to supermarkets, as the product also has the certification of the Ministry of Industry, Science, Technology and Innovation. At the same time, she called for small enterprises to register legally to facilitate access to credit, business expansion and support from relevant ministries.
The spread of Kovid 19 is a time when everyone is thinking about health. This is also an opportunity for Ms. Seng Samnang to start a handicraft that produces powders from plants such as banana powder, turmeric, turmeric, sprouts and other vegetables to sell on the market for those who think about health, eat by production and packaging made by hand.
Ms. Seng Samnang, who is currently the director of JMLH Enterprise in Battambang province, said that after she switched production and manual packaging to machine use, the production has increased in volume and packaging has become more standardized. And attractive while using less labor. The market share is also bigger than bigger.
Ms. Aun Sokunthea, the owner of Kola Oil Company, a family of small-scale fire-breathing dragon oil for more than 10 years, has been expanding into a larger standard business to make this first aid product recognized. From national and international consumers and reach future exports.
Currently, there are more than 750,000 small and medium enterprises in Cambodia, of which more than 44,628 are registered with the Ministry of Industry by 2023.
Veal Veng Reconciliation Center Leads Youth in Military History Study Tour
(Pursat): On the afternoon of July 11, 2024, the working group of Veal Veng Reconciliation Center led 23 youths from Phnom Kravanh district, including 15 women, to visit the military site of Techo Phnom Moan (A4). Along National Road 55 towards the Cambodian-Thai border.
Pursuant to Sub-Decree No. 155 on the Determination of Military History Locations of the Royal Government of Cambodia, signed by Samdech Akka Moha Sena Padei Techo Hun Sen on 11 July 2023, there are a total of 87 military historical sites.
2. My name is Hang Srey Nin, I am a 12th grade student at Hun Sen Phnom Kravanh High School. I became more and more aware of the military historical site of Techo Phnom Moan (A4), Veal Veng, Pursat province, and the area where the Khmer Rouge had set up battlefields, surrounded by mountains, making it difficult for the army of the People's Republic of Kampuchea to invade. In addition, the Khmer Rouge fired artillery at Thai and other enemy aircraft.
3. My name is Tith Srey Nich, female, 21 years old, from Phnom Kravanh District, Pursat Province. I was really excited when I got to know and know about the military history of Techo Phnom Moan (A4) Veal Veng. This location is the bitter historical site of the fighting. As I listen to what is happening in this area, it makes me want to know more about the history of Cambodia.
