After giving up farming for five years, the 55-year-old farmer returned to growing vegetables. But in return, he did not choose to grow with chemicals, researching how to grow crops that do not affect the health of consumers as well as himself. That is why he decided to choose agro-ecological farming practices.
Currently, Mr. Siv Nouth grows vegetables such as kale, kale, kale, lettuce and some other fruits and vegetables in a row. He said that in this way, his family can harvest and sell every day, get a regular income in all seasons and not worry about leaving crops in the field.
There are five children in the care of Mr. Siv Noeut, who used to be a farmer who has a difficult life and health problems due to growing crops using chemicals, now has a better life.
Agro-ecology is an agricultural production system that values biodiversity and improves the overall food system, maintains sustainable soil quality, as well as improves the environment and society as well as the economy of farmers and their communities through Transformation from chemical farming to organic farming techniques, multi-crop farming and animal husbandry.
He emphasized that ALiSEA is a network of stakeholders such as the private sector, government, NGOs, researchers and farmers' organizations in order to raise awareness of agro-ecological practices. The regional ALiSEA network consists of four countries: Cambodia, Laos, Vietnam and Myanmar, with a total of 238 members.
The Agro-Ecology Network (ALiSEA) aims to strengthen the capacity of stakeholders by exchanging knowledge and sharing experiences on agro-ecological practices, increasing the visibility and credibility of agro-ecological policy makers and consumers of agro-ecological products. And increase the development and adoption of eco-agriculture practices among farmers.
