Global oil market supply to be lower than next year
NEW YORK (Reuters) - Global demand for crude oil will exceed supply next year, contrary to previous estimates, the Energy Information Administration said on Tuesday. According to Reuters.
Reuters reports that the change in forecast comes after OPEC and its ally, OPEC +, extended their oil output cuts until next year at a meeting last month. The group has been tightening yields since late 2022 to bolster oil markets amid falling demand, high interest rates and a sharp drop in US output.
If the market falls into a deficit, refiners will be forced to extract crude oil from refineries to meet demand. Removing crude oil from the stockpile will push up oil prices.
In its previous forecast, the EIA estimated global demand for oil at about 104.5 million barrels a day and supply at 104.7 million barrels.
EIA forecasts show that OPEC + lower yields are also widening the supply deficit for the rest of the year. According to the report, global oil demand will exceed oil production by about 750,000 barrels per day in the second half of 2024. But previous estimates put the deficit at about 550,000 barrels in the second half of this year. According to Reuters.
