Pre-departure orientation sessions are designed to ensure that civil servants are well-prepared to face the cultural and social differences they may encounter during their study and training in South Korea. . By providing them with the knowledge and culture of intercultural communication skills and an understanding of study expectations, this oriented program becomes a stepping stone to successful and empowering experiences for civil servants.
With a mission to support human resource development (HRD) in partner countries, KOICA invites talented people from Cambodia and helps them gain professional knowledge that will play an important role in Cambodia.
KOICA Scholarship Program (CIAT) is Korea's leading human resource development (HRD) training program. Through the program, KOICA regularly invites civil servants, engineers, researchers and policy makers from developing countries to provide capacity building and training in various fields, focusing on sharing experiences and technologies of Korean development.
In the pre-departure orientation session, Ms. Thorn Kheng Vichrani, a former student of the KOICA Scholarship Program from Cambodia, who is currently working as an Assistance Coordinator at the Council for the Development of Cambodia (CDC), shared her experience gained from her scholarship in the country. Korea and how she is applying knowledge in her current role as an aid coordinator.
Officials who attended the pre-departure orientation session expressed their gratitude and assurance of their support to KOICA and the Korean government and pledged to bring what they would learn from Korea to help. To the development of Cambodia.
It should be noted that since 1994, the KOICA Scholarship Program has supported Cambodian civil servants with a total of 3,647 participants in various assistance projects such as national training programs, global training programs and scholarship programs. KOICA will continue to support and implement programs in Cambodia that target human resource development.
Veal Veng Reconciliation Center Distributes Wheelchairs and Earphones to Disabled Khmer Rouge Survivors in Veal Veng District, Pursat Province
(Pursat): The team of Veal Veng Reconciliation Center on July 9 and 10, 2024 visited Bramoy village, Stung Thmey village and Chheuteal Chrum village in Bramoy commune, Veal Veng district, Pursat province to provide wheelchairs and Earphones for four Khmer Rouge survivors with disabilities
Later, Mr. Sotda, 52, Chief of Stung Thmey Village, said that the distribution of wheelchairs and hearing aids to the disabled survivors of the Khmer Rouge regime was very beneficial because he could travel and work. Little around the house easier.
"I am happy that the Veal Veng Reconciliation Center team has contributed and taken care of the disabled people who are survivors of the Khmer Rouge regime in my village," Sotda said. "The distribution of wheelchairs and hearing aids has eased the burden on the families of people with disabilities to a greater extent and facilitated better communication with neighbors."
After listening to Soda's remarks, the Veal Veng Reconciliation Center team distributed wheelchairs and earplugs to disabled Khmer Rouge survivors in Bramoy commune.
