JAKARTA: Indonesia's budget deficit this year is estimated at 2.7 percent of gross domestic product, higher than the government's target of 2.29 percent as state spending is expected to rise. Rising as the rupee depreciates. According to Reuters.
The Indonesian rupiah depreciated to 16,475 against the dollar last month, the lowest level in four years, due to a rise in the value of the dollar and concerns over the new government's spending plan. In the future. The currency fell about 6.3 percent in the first half of the year.
Government spending on fuel subsidies has risen as the rupee has depreciated, but Finance Minister Sri Mulyani Indrawati said the government would be careful in managing its debt and optimizing the use of excess cash. Its from last year's budget.
She said the government would spend about 100 trillion rupees ($ 6.15 billion) more than last year's target to reduce debt this year. "Hopefully this will help maintain macroeconomic stability, especially exchange rate changes and government bond yields," she told the Indonesian Parliamentary Budget Committee.
In the first six months of 2024, the Indonesian government announced a fiscal deficit of 0.34 percent. According to Reuters.
