Minister Sok Soken and Mr. Lou Meng chaired a meeting of the Tourism Working Group (Group B) of the Royal Government-Private Sector Forum
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Sok Soken, Minister of Tourism and Chairman of the Tourism Working Group and Oknha Lou Meng, Co-Chair of the Tourism Working Group, on July 9, 2024, chaired the Tourism Working Group of the Royal Government-Private Forum. Attended by the Vice-Chairs, members of the Tourism Working Group, both public and private, and a total of 100 Secretariat teams at the Ministry of Tourism.
The purpose of this meeting is to: 1. Monitor the progress of the implementation of the results of the 19th Royal Government-Private Sector Forum, which the Royal Government has entrusted to the working group on tourism. 2. Discuss and provide solutions to new challenges. Additional of the private sector proposed by the Cambodian Chamber of Commerce and 3- Summarize the report to the Coordinating Committee for the Royal Government-Private Sector Forum Mechanism.
The meeting confirmed that the 19th Royal Government-Private Sector Forum has assigned the Working Group on Tourism (Group B) 22 measures, so far Working Group B has fully resolved 11 points, equivalent to 50%. And 11 other measures are being promoted.
Separately, the Cambodian Chamber of Commerce also sent 10 additional solutions, equivalent to 19 suggestions, to Working Group B for further discussion and resolution to further boost tourism growth.
The meeting also held discussions with the participation of inter-institutional opinions and interpretations of the private sector to achieve a number of challenges that need to be addressed in the tourism working group mechanism and some other challenges. At the Royal Government level.
In addition, the meeting also decided to define the roles and responsibilities of inter-institutions to take on the task of solving challenges under their jurisdiction and to facilitate the implementation of monitoring and evaluation mechanisms and be able to summarize the results of the response to the private sector and report to the Royal Government. .
