China's total foreign exchange reserves fall in June
BEIJING (Reuters) - China's foreign exchange reserves stood at 3.22 trillion US dollars as of June 2024, official data showed on Sunday. According to China's Xinhua news agency.
The amount was down 0.3 percent from the beginning of June due to fluctuations in currency and asset prices, Xinhua reported, citing data released by the Foreign Exchange Administration (SAFE).
The SAFE administration explained that due to factors such as monetary policy of major economies, market expectations and macroeconomic data, the US dollar index and global financial asset prices rose in June.
The exchange rate regulator said that the Chinese economy has maintained its momentum of recovery and continued high-quality development, which is conducive to maintaining a stable level of China's foreign exchange reserves.
China's gold reserves remained unchanged in June at 72.8 million ounces, according to data released on Sunday by the People's Bank of China, the People's Bank of China. About $ 169.7 billion.
