(Siem Reap): The largest silk handicraft in Puok district, Siem Reap province, on July 8, 2024, held a blessing ceremony to reopen after a temporary suspension due to the Kovid 19 crisis.
During the ceremony, Mr. Kay Lot, Chairman of the Board, said that this silk handicraft supports and preserves the cultural heritage and ancient handicrafts of Cambodia and has a positive impact on the lives of people at the grassroots level.
He said that this is a reunion of the family of Angkor silk handicrafts after the disappearance of Kovid 19 disease, so we gathered to work staff and artisans in wood carving, stone carving and 180 silk weaving. People.
Mr. Kay Lot said that before the 19-Kovid crisis, Angkor silk handicrafts in Sot Puok had more than 400 artisans, and the first phase recruited 180 people when the silk handicrafts went well and continued to gather old artisans to work. Repeat.
Mr. Pao Ieng Khong, Director of Angkot Silk Handicraft in Sot Puok, said that this silk handicraft was disrupted during the world with the 19 Kovid crisis and after the Kovid 19 crisis disappeared, the silk handicraft was rehabilitated. To be officially re-launched, regrouping 180 skilled craftsmen to return to work after a large number of them left the profession.
Mr. Pav Ieng Khong continued that the silk sector has been strengthened and preserved by the Royal Government to preserve the skills of the ancestors in silk production. On the other hand, this silk weaving skill is the legacy of our Khmer ancestors who left and inscribed on the temples, which is the skill of our Khmer ancestors for centuries as well.
