The Cambodian Red Cross launches a campaign to plant 1,000 trees in the Angkor resort area along Norodom Sahamoni Blvd. to celebrate the 72nd anniversary of the 9th July 2024 Tree Festival
The ceremony was held in the presence of Mr. Pit Chamnan, Member of the Central Committee of the Cambodian Red Cross and Permanent Vice President of the Youth Activities Support Group and CRC Volunteers, and Ms. Tuy Chi Prak Sophoan, Vice Chairman of the Provincial Branch Committee of the Cambodian Red Cross. Attended by Mr. and Mrs. Executives, Strategic Advisors of the Cambodian Red Cross Branch from 6 provinces as well as 23 higher education institutions in Phnom Penh.
Speaking on the occasion, Ms. Tuy Chi Prak Sophoan said that trees are the life of humanity and animals. On the other hand, planting trees now is a reflection of our active efforts to restore green forest cover to sustain climate change.
She said trees play an important role in maintaining ecosystems by helping to regulate climate change, reducing the negative effects of climate change, such as floods, hurricanes and the absorption of carbon dioxide from the atmosphere. Authorities at all levels and the people take care of it regularly so that the seedlings grow fast and are not lost because the forest is the life of all of us.
He added that this is not the first time that the Cambodian Red Cross and its branches have planted a series of trees, including: The Cambodian Red Cross Millennium Park on an area of 3 hectares with 2,460 trees. Siem Reap has taken care of this park, which is a natural resource to protect the environment of Angkor.
Mr. Pit Chamnan added that the park was officially inaugurated and planted 2,000 trees on the occasion of World Red Cross Red Crescent Day 1999 under the high presidency of Queen Norodom Monineath Sihanouk, the Queen Mother of Cambodia in Freedom, Dignity and Happiness, Honorary President of the Cambodian Red Cross. The tree planting campaign will contribute to the natural habitat and further protect the environment of Angkor.
Mr. Pit Chamnan stated that we cannot solve the crisis alone, but must be able to protect our lives and human rights now and in the future by making a commitment to reduce emissions, stop environmental degradation and Reduce the use of plastic bags and adapt to increased risks.
He stated that today, young people of the Cambodian Red Cross from the provinces to participate in the tree planting campaign to honor the planting of 1,000 trees every year to conserve and manage forests sustainably. It is important to plant trees in the Angkor area to maintain the environmental beauty of the Angkor resort.
At the same time, he also advised the students and youth of the Cambodian Red Cross to be virtuous, moral and humane to help the society and participate in the preservation of natural resources. Dr. Kun Nhim distributed 1,500 saplings to the district administration. Kien Svay to be distributed to all 8 communes to be planted at pagodas, schools, health centers and public places
