Today marks the 16th anniversary of the inclusion of the Temple of Preah Vihear in the World Heritage List
The decision to inscribe Cambodia's treasures on the World Heritage List was made at the 32nd session in Quebec City, Canada. The decision of UNESCO is a great success of the Royal Government of Cambodia.
In the cause of upholding national cultural heritage and defending national sovereignty, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, then Prime Minister of Cambodia, sent a letter to UNESCO in 2001 requesting the registration of the Temple of Preah Vihear as a World Heritage Site. Samdech Techo once said that the inclusion of the Temple of Preah Vihear in the World Heritage List is in accordance with the planned plan, which is a priority of Cambodia's foreign policy.
A year later, UNESCO responded positively in helping to prepare documents for the inclusion of the Temple of Preah Vihear as a World Heritage Site. In 2006, the World Heritage Committee agreed to accept Cambodia's request and sent a team of experts to inspect the condition of the temple in 2007.
After a thorough study and evaluation, at the 32nd meeting in Quebec City, Canada on July 7, 2008, the UNESCO World Heritage Committee decided to include the Temple of Preah Vihear on the World Heritage List.
The decision by UNESCO to designate the Temple of Preah Vihear as a World Heritage Site is a great achievement for Cambodia under the leadership of Cambodian Prime Minister Samdech Techo Hun Sen in claiming ownership of the Temple of Preah Vihear after regaining control. From Thailand on June 15, 1962 under the great crusade of King Norodom Sihanouk.
This historic success has brought immense joy and excitement to Cambodians all over Cambodia, including abroad, cheering, burning candles, playing drums and so on.
The Royal Government of Cambodia, led by Samdech Techo and special envoys such as Samdech Vibol Panha Sok An, has worked hard to prepare joint documents and other procedures in accordance with UNESCO requirements. Just days after the temple was inscribed on the World Heritage List, Thailand raised troops to invade Cambodian territory in the area of the Preah Vihear temple.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen Khmer Rouge, who was determined to defend the country and the territory, fought hard by all means, including the armed forces and the means of international law, to protect this precious property of Cambodia from being lost.
To celebrate this great achievement, the Royal Government of Cambodia has designated July 10 as the National Day of Remembrance for the inclusion of the Temple of Preah Vihear and Sambor Prei Kuk in the World Heritage List and recommended the celebration. Even more so.
The temple of Preah Vihear is 405 km from Phnom Penh, but if you travel from the provincial town of Preah Vihear, it is only 108 km by road 211 to Choam Ksan district. At Phnom Dangrek, there is a ladder that allows one to reach the top of the mountain, where the temple of Preah Vihear is located.
According to research from various Khmer historical documents, the temple of Preah Vihear has been built of stone since the reign of King Suryavarman I. Suryavarman I reigned in the first half of the 11th century. The site of the highest temple is 625 meters (French documents) or 615 meters (English documents).
The history of the construction of the Temple of Preah Vihear is found on the Khmer inscription at Baphuon Temple in Siem Reap Province, No. 583: They are written in Sanskrit and Khmer.
It is written that there was a prince named Indrayuth who took a penis named Srey Sikhak Risvara and resided here at the temple of Preah Vihear. It is written that he was very strong. He had the power to go to war with the Cham king and capture that Cham king again.
As for this inscription, historians think that no other inscription mentions a war to capture the Cham king, but the king he captured was probably not the Cham king who ruled the entire Cham country. . Probably a small king who ruled a kingdom bordering Cambodia.
