Jean-Pierre Lacroix also praised and praised Cambodia's success in transforming itself from a country with a UN presence into a country that sends active participation in UN peacekeeping missions. Having shared professional skills in practice, especially in the task of clearing mines and destroying unexploded ordnance.
General Sem Sovanny, Director General of the National Peacekeeping Center, welcomed the visit and said that in 2006, Cambodia began to move forward by sending troops to the Republic of Sudan for the first time, and in 2024 it will have 18 years of mission. UN peacekeepers in Cambodia have sent a total of 9,579 troops, including 818 women, to 10 countries and 12 missions.
General Sem Sovanny added that Cambodia is currently prominent in sending three female commanders to the mission areas, including the Central African Republic, Lebanon and South Sudan. At the same time, the rate of sending Cambodian women to the mission area is about 17.2 percent, with the general also committed to increase the number of women soldiers.
