The German Ambassador confirmed this in a meeting with Samdech Techo Hun Sen, President of the Senate of Cambodia this morning, Thursday, July 4, 2024.
Welcoming the meeting, Samdech Techo congratulated the German government for hosting the European Football Championship well and congratulated the German football team on their success in the quarter-finals.
The German Ambassador noted that during his two years of service in Cambodia, he had seen Cambodia's progress, especially Phnom Penh's rapid and vibrant development.
At the same time, the Ambassador also expressed his pleasure at the continuous development of relations and cooperation between Cambodia and Germany, especially the strengthening of people-to-people exchanges and exchanges. According to the ambassador, over the past two years, up to 17 German delegations have visited Cambodia, including the president and his wife. This is more than 10 years ago.
The ambassador also said that in the future, three more German business delegations will visit Cambodia and he hopes that German direct investment will be further expanded in Cambodia.
The Ambassador also briefed Samdech Techo on the opening of the German Honorary Consulate in Siem Reap last June, led by an Honorary Consul specializing in trade and tourism. The Ambassador believes that the Honorary Consulate will help attract German and other European countries to visit Siem Reap, which is rich in cultural heritage.
The German ambassador added that he would continue to support the efforts of the seventh government to develop in line with the Pentagon strategy.
In addition to bilateral relations, the German ambassador said that Cambodia and Germany have good cooperation in international forums. The Ambassador also thanked Cambodia for supporting the training of Ukrainian deminers. Germany wants Cambodia to continue to expand this training.
Regarding this demining issue, the Ambassador promised to continue working with various organizations on demining so that Cambodia can achieve its goal of becoming a demining-free country by 2025. With this, Germany also continues to support the project to preserve the temples of Angkor and other temples, which are rich cultural heritage, which is the longest project in the world.
Samdech Techo, President of the Senate, expressed his pleasure that he has worked continuously with the German leadership, especially during the recent meeting with the German President in Brussels, and the two leaders agreed to continue working together.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen thanked the German government for its continued assistance to Cambodia in socio-economic development. Samdech Techo said that the exchange of high-level delegations has further enhanced the cooperation between the two countries, especially in the fields of trade, investment and tourism, which has made remarkable progress. Samdech Techo continued that Samdech is proud of the good achievements achieved together.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen requested the German Ambassador to continue promoting economic, investment and human resource development.
Regarding the issue of mines, Samdech Techo, President of the Senate, emphasized that Cambodia plans to complete all demining by 2025, but Samdech is concerned that this goal may not be achieved completely. Samdech Techo hopes that Germany will continue to help with demining.
Samdech Techo also thanked the German side for taking care of and helping to maintain Cambodia's temples. As Cambodia has many temples, Samdech Techo calls on the German government and other friends to continue to help protect, preserve and preserve this ancestral heritage.
On bilateral relations, Samdech Techo Senate President asked the German side to push for negotiations to reach a free trade agreement between ASEAN and the European Union.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen expressed his pleasure to continue the work done together, especially to promote German tourism to Cambodia even more. Samdech Techo believes that the establishment of a German Honorary Consulate in Siem Reap may also help boost tourism.
During the meeting, Samdech Techo and the German Ambassador also discussed regional and global challenges, including the political crisis in Myanmar and the war in Ukraine.
