World Bank cuts Thai growth forecast for this year and next
BANGKOK: Thailand's economy is expected to grow 2.4 percent this year, down from its April forecast of 2.8 percent, the World Bank (WB) said on Wednesday. The decline was largely due to lower-than-expected exports and public investment earlier this year.
The growth of Southeast Asia's second-largest economy will be driven by consumer spending, Reuters reported, citing a press release issued by the World Bank. Tourism industry and export recovery. Last year, the Thai economy grew 1.9 percent, slower than other countries in the region.
The World Bank says foreign tourist arrivals are expected to reach 36.1 million this year, up from 28.2 million in 2023 and close to the peak received. Before the epidemic.
The total number of foreign tourist arrivals by 2025 is projected to reach 41.1 million by 2025, exceeding pre-existing levels, according to Reuters. Outbreaks appear to be exacerbated by the influx of Chinese tourists.
