The Royal Government of Cambodia started the school feeding program in 1999, starting with the implementation of the World Food Program-sponsored breakfast program. It then launched a food scholarship program in 2004 and piloted a school-based school feeding program using community-based agricultural products in 2014, as well as a school lunch program in 2017.
Dr. John Narith, Secretary General of the National Council for Social Protection, said that in 2024, the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports has been implementing school feeding programs using community agriculture in 553 target schools in the province. 10: Siem Reap, Kampong Thom, Preah Vihear, Battambang, Stung Treng, Banteay Meanchey, Kampong Cham, Kampong Chhnang, Pursat and Oddar Meanchey, including 153,586 students and 1,518 chefs.
At the same time, the Secretary-General of the National Council for Social Protection stated that the school-based school-based school feeding program aims to support the physical and mental growth in access to education through child nutrition. In areas with high food insecurity and low levels of education, as well as increasing enrollment rates and reducing primary school dropout rates.
"Overall, the school feeding program has been providing nutrition to children of all school-age communities in poor and vulnerable families facing food insecurity," he said. In addition, it also contributes to promoting equitable enrollment, enhancing the efficiency and quality of education and increasing the participation of all stakeholders.
"We want our children to grow up to be well-nourished because of the food, because the food we cook at this school is balanced with nutrients to provide enough calories for the students and to increase their ability to absorb knowledge because when they From home still hungry can not learn to enter, so until the students eat a full meal in the morning. This is an additional emphasis of Dr. John Narith.
Dr. John Narith added that since the beginning of the program, enrollment rates have risen and dropout rates are almost non-existent for target schools due to attendance, but they are being accepted. Extra encouragement by providing breakfast to eat as part of reducing family expenses.
It should be informed that the school feeding program is part of improving the quality of education by using agricultural products in the community, which aims to improve the nutrition and education of children in areas of high poverty and vulnerability by contributing to increasing Enroll, and reduce dropout rates at the primary level, as well as contribute to the community economy.
